      "widgets_values": [
        "# 02 \u00b7 control/structure\n\nThe workhorse. Takes an image read as **mass, depth and arrangement** and renders a new picture on that scaffolding.\n\n## How it differs from sketch\n\nSketch sees edges. Structure sees *volume* \u2014 where things are, how big, how far back, how the tones fall across them. So it keeps considerably more of the original than sketch does: silhouette, weight, spatial depth, the general distribution of light and dark.\n\nThat makes it the right endpoint when a card is nearly there and you want it re-rendered rather than re-drawn. It is also the one to reach for when sketch keeps losing the subject: sketch throws away everything sketch cannot see as a line, and a soft-edged painting has very few.\n\n## The dial\n\n`control_strength` 0-1, and it behaves differently here than it does on sketch.\n\n- **0.3** \u2014 a loose echo. Same rough arrangement, freely reinterpreted.\n- **0.7** \u2014 the default, and usually right. Clearly the same image, restated.\n- **0.9** \u2014 close to a re-render of the original. Little room for the prompt.\n\nThe failure at the top of the range is not rigidity but *futility*: at 0.95 the prompt has almost nothing left to change, and you have paid for a copy. If the result ignores your prompt, the dial is too high \u2014 that is the first thing to check, before rewriting the prompt.\n\n## Chaining these four\n\nThey all share one convention, and that is the whole mechanism \u2014 there is no workflow-calls-a-workflow in ComfyUI, so a folder is the join:\n\n```\nstability/in/              drop your sources here\nstability/out/sketch/      <- 01 writes here\nstability/out/structure/   <- 02\nstability/out/style/       <- 03\nstability/out/transfer/    <- 04\n```\n\nEvery one of these graphs reads **the newest image** in its `directory` (`sort_by = modified`, `descending`, `limit = 1`). So to feed one endpoint's output into another, change this graph's `directory` to that graph's output folder and run it. Structure reading `stability/out/sketch` is a two-step chain; nothing else needs rewiring.\n\nThe filename carries through: `Load Images From Directory` emits `filenames`, which is wired to the save node's `label`, so `the-fool.png` stays `the-fool.png` at every stage and you can always tell which source a result came from. `overwrite` is off, so a second run lands as `the-fool_1.png` rather than destroying the first \u2014 and \"newest by modified\" still picks it up.\n\nEach result gets a sidecar JSON with the prompt and settings that made it.\n\n## Cost\n\nEach of the three nodes below is **one paid call**, so a run is three. That is the point of a study graph \u2014 you are buying the comparison, not the image. **Ctrl+B** any of them to drop it from the run once you know which end of the dial you care about; bypass passes the input straight through and costs nothing.\n\nSeeds are fixed at **7**. Stability treats seed 0 as \"pick a random one\", which would make the three results incomparable and stop any of them being cached. With the seed pinned, the dial is the only thing that differs between them.\n\n## The prompt lives in one node\n\nThe **Shared Prompt** node feeds every `prompt` field in this graph \u2014 all three settings \u2014 and their `negative` too, so there is one\nplace to edit and the copies cannot drift apart. The comparison is only honest while every setting is reading the same words.\n\nIt emits a plain string, **not** a list. That is the difference between it and **Prompt\nList**, which would make each node it feeds run once per line and multiply what the graph\ncosts."
      ],
      "title": "Read me \u2014 control/structure",
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
